      "rev_n_1_afe1f2_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_afe1f2_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_afe1f2",
        "thought_text": "Iterative Division with Correct Termination Condition\n\nThought-1 (revised): Instead of incrementing `n` and checking if `math.factorial(n)` exceeds the given factorial value, we can use iterative division to reduce the factorial value step-by-step. This approach ensures that we accurately determine the value of `n` without prematurely stopping the loop. We will divide the factorial value by increasing values of `n` starting from 1 until the factorial value becomes less than `n`. The last valid `n` before this condition is met will be our answer.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_afe1f2_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_944bcdecb2",
        "created_at": 1794505377.377047
      },
      "rev_n_3_d2c61e_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_d2c61e_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_d2c61e",
        "thought_text": "Binary search is not efficient enough for this problem due to the large range of possible values for \\( n \\). Instead, we can use logarithms to estimate the upper bound for \\( n \\) more accurately and then perform a linear search within a reasonable range. This approach will significantly reduce the time complexity.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_d2c61e_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_b51950ffb0",
        "created_at": 1794505394.6346116
      },
      "rev_n_4_7412ba_0": {
